WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.118 --> 00:00:02.160
SPEAKER: In this episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.950
we're going to dive deep
into how to ask a question

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.680
or run a query in BigQuery.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.260
Running queries is one of
the most fundamental parts

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.960
of discovering insights
from your data.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.510
Today's question--
what is the best jersey

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.610
number you should choose
in order to improve

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.170
your basketball game?

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.650
Stay tuned and find out.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.506
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.470
It's no surprise
that big data has

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.380
made its way into the world
of professional sports.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.120
Teams at every
level are starting

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.600
to gather, process,
and analyze data

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.700
so that they can get the
most out of their players

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.170
and find a competitive
edge in the game.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.180
To see how this would
work, let's check out

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.650
the NCAA basketball public
data set available in BigQuery.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.170
The data set contains
play-by-play data

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.810
for several years'
worth of games.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.680
You can get information like
fouls, free throws, scores,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.220
player numbers, timeouts,
basically as much data

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.180
as the pros have.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.910
So let's see if we can use
this data for today's question

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.475
and determine which
jersey number is the best

00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:11.380
three-point shooter.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.240
If you want to
follow along, click

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.100
on the link in the
description below

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.290
to set up your own
BigQuery sandbox

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.710
where you can analyze data
without needing a credit card.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.480
Once you're in the
BigQuery sandbox,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.820
head over to the Google
Cloud Marketplace

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.650
by clicking on Add Data
and then Explore Data Sets.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.920
Search for the NCAA
basketball and click

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.180
to launch the data set
in the BigQuery UI.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.180
The NCAA basketball data set has
a number of different tables.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.400
Clicking on the
play-by-play table,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.350
you can first view
the table details,

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.240
which indicates that
there are over four

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.400
million rows of events.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.990
That's a lot of basketball data.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.700
Check the table
schema, and you'll

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.060
see each column or field of
data that's available for each

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.390
of those events.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.420
But first, let's look at
what those events are.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.940
You can start to
explore the data

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.310
by clicking on the
Preview tab to view

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.150
a few rows of sample data.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.600
But to get a more comprehensive
list of all the event

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.870
types, the best way to
find out is to run a query.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.690
Click Query Table
and a blank query

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.890
will appear in the editor with
a table reference pre-populated.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.290
Before we write our query,
it's important to know

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.480
that the language used to
communicate with BigQuery

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.810
is SQL, or Structured
Query Language.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.730
SQL is also the
standard language

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.940
for communicating with
relational databases.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.900
BigQuery is ANSI SQL compliant,
so experienced practitioners

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.770
will hit the ground running.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.040
Don't worry if
you're not SQL savvy.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.650
There are plenty of resources
for learning or brushing

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.750
up on your SQL skills just
a quick web search away.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.270
So now, let's copy
in a SQL query

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.540
to explore the event types.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.180
The key command in SQL
for retrieving data

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.160
is SELECT, which
we'll use to pull

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.980
in a list of all of the
different event types

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.820
and number of occurrences
from the play-by-play table.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.000
Notice that the table reference
is in the format of the GCP

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.190
project name, then
data set name,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.980
then table name,
separated by dots

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.090
and enclosed with back
ticks, not quotes.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.630
We'll select all of the event
types and group the results

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.060
so that we can get the
count of each event.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.330
Once the SQL query is written,
click on the green check mark

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.980
on the right-hand
side of the window

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.640
to open the query validator.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.900
A green check mark
means the query is valid

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.760
and will show the
estimated amount of data

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.570
that the query will
process when you run it.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:39.110
If the query is invalid, a
red exclamation point icon

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.320
is displayed.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.050
You can click on it
and get some guidance

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.400
on how to correct the problem.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.160
Click Run and
BigQuery gets to work.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.350
After the query
execution is complete,

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.390
the query service
displays the results

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.980
as a table in the web UI.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.160
You can see that the
Event Type column has

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.560
both three point made and
three point miss events.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.330
Now we can write
a new query which

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:06.110
selects the jersey number
for each play-by-play event

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.450
and then uses the
Event Types column

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.460
to determine the
three point field goal

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.950
accuracy for each jersey number.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.079
Click Run and then in less than
a second we have the answer.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.170
Looks like number 45 sinks
the highest percentage

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.120
of their three-pointer attempts.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.190
Of course, buying a
new jersey doesn't

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.180
make you a better
basketball player, right?

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.270
An important note-- we can
see that the table we queried

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.550
has three gigabytes of data.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.280
But BigQuery only read the
columns needed and therefore

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.340
just processed 78
megabytes of data.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.170
Well, basketball
enthusiasts out there,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.720
try out the NCAA
data set for yourself

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.080
and ask questions to
discover what you might

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.952
be able to do to up your game.

